{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Problem\n\n`src/rebar/llm/eval_specs/plan-review-g7.eval.yaml` declares `prompt: plan-review-G7` (line 13), and it is the only packaged spec whose filename differs from its `prompt` field (checked over every `src/rebar/llm/eval_specs/*.eval.yaml` at origin/main dd72b461814). `load_eval_spec(prompt_id)` (`src/rebar/llm/evals/eval.py:71`) opens `<prompt_id>.eval.yaml` verbatim (`_packaged_eval_spec`, `eval.py:63`), and `calibrate_criterion(\"G7\")` maps the criterion to `criterion_prompt_id(\"G7\") == \"plan-review-G7\"`.\n\nSo on a case-sensitive filesystem (Linux, including the GitHub ubuntu runners):\n\n- `calibrate_criterion(\"G7\")` / `rebar criteria eval G7` raise `EvalError: no eval spec for prompt 'plan-review-G7' at .../plan-review-G7.eval.yaml: [Errno 2] No such file or directory`.\n- The `eval-live` loop in `.github/workflows/prompt-eval.yml` takes `pid = spec[\"prompt\"]` (`plan-review-G7`) and calls `run_eval(pid)`, which raises the same uncaught `EvalError` and aborts the step before the specs that sort after it are run.\n\nmacOS's default case-insensitive filesystem hides it.\n\n## Evidence\n\nObserved in Gerrit change 3421 patch set 1 CI (GitHub run 44239093717, ubuntu py3.11/3.12/3.13 and eval-discipline): `tests/unit/test_eval.py::test_calibrate_criterion_opens_a_gate_session_for_its_solver` called `calibrate_criterion(\"G7\", ...)` and failed with exactly that `EvalError`; the same test passes on macOS. That test was switched to the `necessity` criterion so the change could land; the underlying mismatch is unchanged.\n\n## Root cause\n\nThe spec file was committed under a lower-case name while every caller addresses it by its upper-case prompt id. The two other upper-case criterion specs (`plan-review-T10.eval.yaml`, `plan-review-T15.eval.yaml`) are already named after their prompt ids, so the established convention is \"filename == prompt field\", and G7 alone breaks it. Nothing tests that convention.\n\n## Plan\n\n1. Rename the spec with a case-only `git mv` to `src/rebar/llm/eval_specs/plan-review-G7.eval.yaml` (two-step rename through a temporary name so it is recorded correctly on case-insensitive filesystems), and fix its header comment's `rebar prompt eval plan-review-g7` hint to `plan-review-G7`.\n2. Update the callers that relied on the lower-case name: `tests/unit/test_plan_review_g7.py` (`load_eval_spec(\"plan-review-g7\")`) and `tests/unit/test_eval.py` (`run_eval(\"plan-review-g7\", ...)`, three sites), and restore `test_calibrate_criterion_opens_a_gate_session_for_its_solver` to calibrate `G7`, the case that failed on Linux.\n3. Add a regression test in `tests/unit/test_new_eval_specs.py` asserting that, for every packaged `*.eval.yaml`, the filename stem (minus `.eval`) equals the spec's `prompt` field EXACTLY (case-sensitive string comparison, independent of the host filesystem), so a future mismatch fails on macOS, Windows and Linux alike.\n4. Make the `eval-live` loop in `.github/workflows/prompt-eval.yml` survive one bad spec: wrap the per-spec `ev.run_eval(pid, ...)` in `try/except ev.EvalError`, print `::error::` naming the spec and the error, append the pid to `broken`, and continue to the next spec. The existing end-of-step `if broken: raise SystemExit(1)` still fails the job, so a bad spec is reported rather than hidden, but no longer skips the specs after it.\n\nRejected: a case-insensitive loader. It would need a directory scan per load, would make two specs that differ only by case ambiguous on Linux, and would hide the naming drift instead of fixing it.\n\n## Portability\n\nA case-only rename is handled by git on case-insensitive filesystems (macOS APFS default, Windows NTFS): checkout replaces the entry, and after the change both case-sensitive and case-insensitive hosts open `plan-review-G7.eval.yaml` by the exact name callers use. The new test compares Python strings, so it does not depend on host filesystem case behaviour. The workflow edit is inline Python in a Linux-only job and uses no OS-specific API. No subprocess, fd, path-separator or encoding behaviour changes.\n\n## File impact\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/eval_specs/plan-review-g7.eval.yaml` → `src/rebar/llm/eval_specs/plan-review-G7.eval.yaml` (rename + header comment)\n- `tests/unit/test_new_eval_specs.py` (new regression test)\n- `tests/unit/test_plan_review_g7.py`, `tests/unit/test_eval.py` (caller names; calibrate test back to G7)\n- `.github/workflows/prompt-eval.yml` (eval-live per-spec error handling)\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The packaged G7 spec is named `plan-review-G7.eval.yaml`, and `load_eval_spec(\"plan-review-G7\")` and `calibrate_criterion(\"G7\", ...)` load it (covered by `tests/unit/test_plan_review_g7.py` and `tests/unit/test_eval.py::test_calibrate_criterion_opens_a_gate_session_for_its_solver` passing in the Gerrit `Verified` ubuntu cells, which run on a case-sensitive filesystem).\n- [ ] `tests/unit/test_new_eval_specs.py` contains a test asserting every packaged spec's filename stem equals its `prompt` field exactly; it fails against the pre-fix tree (RED) and passes after the rename.\n- [ ] The `eval-live` step in `.github/workflows/prompt-eval.yml` catches `EvalError` per spec, records the spec in `broken`, continues to the remaining specs, and still exits non-zero at the end when any spec is broken.\n- [ ] `make verify` passes on the change, and the change lands through Gerrit with `LLM-Review +1` and `Verified +1`.\n"}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1795025775374625477,"uuid":"6c4e485b-c576-4665-8085-25828bbed3b7"}